253
00:15:11,801 --> 00:15:15,520
bourbon, its neighbor to the south enjoys a unique worldwide

254
00:15:15,521 --> 00:15:18,860
fam. At the Jack Daniels Distillery

255
00:15:18,861 --> 00:15:23,220
in Lynchburg, Tennessee, the whiskey looks like bourbon, but

256
00:15:23,221 --> 00:15:25,800
its distinctive flavor has a hint of sweet charcoal.

257
00:15:25,801 --> 00:15:28,800
Tennessee whiskey is a lot like bourbon, except it's got one

258
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:32,200
extra step, where once it comes out of the still, they pour it

259
00:15:32,300 --> 00:15:36,140
in a tub that's full of charcoal and let it filter through all

260
00:15:36,240 --> 00:15:38,840
the way to the bottom. It takes a long time, but it

261
00:15:38,940 --> 00:15:41,840
makes for a smoother whiskey. That's the theory.

262
00:15:41,940 --> 00:15:45,060
The sugar maple charcoal mellowing is the dividing

263
00:15:45,160 --> 00:15:48,260
line between Tennessee whiskey and bourbon.

264
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:52,460
But both styles ultimately end up in the same place, a charred

265
00:15:52,461 --> 00:15:57,060
American white oak barrel. The process begins when the 52

266
00:15:57,061 --> 00:16:00,700
gallon oak barrels are purchased from a cooperage.

267
00:16:00,701 --> 00:16:05,100
These barrel makers assemble the staves and fire char the inside

268
00:16:05,101 --> 00:16:08,700
where the charred wood makes contact with the whiskey.

269
00:16:08,701 --> 00:16:12,620
You can see the char here. This is called an alligator char.

270
00:16:12,621 --> 00:16:16,060
The pattern sort of tends to resemble alligator skin. When

271
00:16:16,061 --> 00:16:21,060
we put the bourbon up in this barrel, the changes of temperature

272
00:16:21,061 --> 00:16:26,140
temperature through the day will actually force the whiskey in and out of the walls of barrel

273
00:16:26,141 --> 00:16:31,348
through that jar. It is essentially a living, breathing thing.

274
00:16:31,349 --> 00:16:33,700
The barrel allows it contact

275
00:16:33,701 --> 00:16:34,487
with air.

276
00:16:34,488 --> 00:16:41,920
And the barrel, because it's porous, in the warmth of the summer expands and breathes

277
00:16:41,921 --> 00:16:48,116
out. And then when the weather turns cold, the barrel contracts and breathes in.

278
00:16:48,117 --> 00:16:49,200
There are some

279
00:16:49,201 --> 00:16:51,440
different wood chemicals that get dissolved.

280
00:16:51,540 --> 00:16:54,340
There are six that taste and smell like vanilla.

281
00:16:54,440 --> 00:16:56,000
There's a number of wood sugars.

282
00:16:56,100 --> 00:16:57,100
There's caramel.

283
00:16:57,200 --> 00:16:59,940
There's tannins and smoke and all that that get dissolved

284
00:17:00,040 --> 00:17:01,840
when it gets hot.

285
00:17:01,940 --> 00:17:03,940
Because the sun and the wind are so critical

286
00:17:04,040 --> 00:17:07,860
to good aging, distilleries are particular about the location

287
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:10,860
and architecture of the barrel house.

288
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:15,200
At Chimbim, some contain over 20,000 whiskey barrels

289
00:17:15,300 --> 00:17:19,200
and reach a height of nine stories.

290
00:17:19,201 --> 00:17:22,400
The aging characteristics high in the house are very much

291
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:25,500
different than they are down here on the first floor.

292
00:17:25,600 --> 00:17:27,800
Up there, temperatures are higher,

293
00:17:27,900 --> 00:17:30,340
conditions are more extreme, and the whiskey will actually

294
00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:33,840
pick up color and flavor quicker and high in the house

295
00:17:33,940 --> 00:17:39,160
than they do down here. After aging a minimum of two years,

296
00:17:39,260 --> 00:17:42,020
the individual whiskey barrels have different color and taste

297
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:45,600
characteristics. Most distilleries then co-mingle

298
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:47,900
the barrels to obtain a consistent product for

299
00:17:47,901 --> 00:17:53,500
bottling. But at Maker's Mark, a process

300
00:17:53,501 --> 00:17:56,700
called barrel rotation is practiced to avoid having any

301
00:17:56,701 --> 00:18:01,140
two barrels age differently. After they've been up in the

302
00:18:01,141 --> 00:18:03,740
top of the warehouse for three summers, the tasting panel

303
00:18:03,741 --> 00:18:06,260
votes on it. And if it's achieved enough

304
00:18:06,261 --> 00:18:10,760
maturity, the ensuing fall will move those barrels to a lower

305
00:18:10,761 --> 00:18:13,160
spot in the warehouse to complete their aging.

306
00:18:13,161 --> 00:18:17,900
And we think that that gives us a much more uniform product.

307
00:18:17,901 --> 00:18:20,540
These barrels are also commingled and then filtered

308
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:21,440
before bottling.

309
00:18:24,440 --> 00:18:27,140
While bourbon remains the true spirit of America,

310
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:30,340
it's not the world's top selling whiskey.

311
00:18:30,440 --> 00:18:32,940
Across the sea, Scottish distillers

312
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:36,960
use fire and smoke to produce a whiskey so good,

313
00:18:37,060 --> 00:18:41,960
they say angels demand their own share.

314
00:18:42,060 --> 00:18:44,360
For a whiskey to be called straight bourbon,

315
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:47,200
some of the requirements are that it be made in the US

316
00:18:47,201 --> 00:18:51,600
contain at least 51% corn and be aged for at least two years

317
00:18:51,601 --> 00:18:53,440
in charred new oak barrels.

318
00:18:58,640 --> 00:19:02,320
In Kentucky bourbon country, a charred American white oak

319
00:19:02,321 --> 00:19:05,560
barrel loses its usefulness after aging just one batch of

320
00:19:05,561 --> 00:19:06,360
whiskey.

321
00:19:08,360 --> 00:19:12,160
But instead of being discarded, the barrels are disassembled

322
00:19:12,161 --> 00:19:13,760
and sold.

323
00:19:13,761 --> 00:19:16,560
In Scotland, Scotch whiskey distillers

324
00:19:16,660 --> 00:19:20,560
eagerly purchase the used bourbon barrels.

325
00:19:20,660 --> 00:19:23,500
The casks that they use are made out

326
00:19:23,600 --> 00:19:25,900
of that beautiful American white oak.

327
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,380
It's still got enough wood sugars in the white oak

328
00:19:29,480 --> 00:19:33,480
to give Scotch both color and lovely gentle honey,

329
00:19:33,580 --> 00:19:36,880
vanilla-like flavors.

330
00:19:36,980 --> 00:19:38,560
Beyond the barrel, the similarities

331
00:19:38,660 --> 00:19:41,180
between American bourbon whiskey and Scotch whiskey

332
00:19:41,280 --> 00:19:43,760
come to an end.

333
00:19:43,761 --> 00:19:48,760
While corn is the primary grain and bourbon, Scotch distillers

334
00:19:48,761 --> 00:19:52,760
use only 100% barley. Everyone in Scotland would

335
00:19:52,761 --> 00:19:56,760
probably deny it, but it's believed that distilling came

336
00:19:56,761 --> 00:20:00,760
to Scotland from the Irish, and it's believed the monks would

337
00:20:00,761 --> 00:20:03,760
have brought across the secrets of distilling to Scotland.

338
00:20:03,761 --> 00:20:08,760
Legally, Scotch is produced only in Scotland.

339
00:20:08,761 --> 00:20:11,760
If the Scotch is made from pure malted barley at a single

340
00:20:11,761 --> 00:20:16,000
barley at a single distillery, it's known as single malt scotch.

341
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:25,670
I have a smoky, peaty one here because they come in all different styles.

342
00:20:25,671 --> 00:20:26,720
And this one is

343
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:29,947
heavy, smoky, and peaty the way the Scots like it.

344
00:20:29,948 --> 00:20:32,240
And I would suggest that you drink it neat.

345
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:36,880
But a good master distiller will tell you that if you put a few drops of water,

346
00:20:36,881 --> 00:20:40,480
that water will open up the aromas and the secrets that are locked inside of your scotch.

347
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:46,760
On Islay, a remote island on Scotland's west coast,

348
00:20:46,860 --> 00:20:49,760
the whiskies have the smokiest flavor in all of Scotland.

349
00:20:53,340 --> 00:20:57,300
For nearly two centuries, Islay's Laphroaig distillery

350
00:20:57,400 --> 00:21:00,480
has achieved its trademark smokiness through a tradition

351
00:21:00,580 --> 00:21:03,580
known as barley floor malting.

352
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:07,060
Well, there are three raw materials made in making whisky,

353
00:21:07,160 --> 00:21:08,880
and barley is the first one of them.

354
00:21:08,980 --> 00:21:11,760
We need barley, we need water, and we need water.

355
00:21:11,761 --> 00:21:14,600
without barley, we would have no material that we could use

356
00:21:14,700 --> 00:21:18,560
to extract the sugar that we're then going to ferment.

357
00:21:18,660 --> 00:21:22,600
Malting is the process of forcing barley to germinate.

358
00:21:22,700 --> 00:21:25,500
Laphroaig is one of a handful of single malt distillers

359
00:21:25,600 --> 00:21:27,700
that performs floor malting on premise.

360
00:21:30,820 --> 00:21:33,620
When the barley arrives, it's first steeped in water

361
00:21:33,720 --> 00:21:38,720
to a moisture content of 45%.

362
00:21:38,820 --> 00:21:40,720
Approximately six tons of the grain

363
00:21:40,721 --> 00:21:45,720
is then spread onto the malting floor to begin drying.

364
00:21:45,820 --> 00:21:47,720
We're actually trying to trick the barley grain

365
00:21:47,820 --> 00:21:50,460
into thinking it's spring, and it's time to germinate

366
00:21:50,560 --> 00:21:52,460
and grow into a new barley plant.

367
00:21:52,560 --> 00:21:55,360
It's developing enzymes, and these enzymes

368
00:21:55,460 --> 00:21:56,960
we can use later on in the process.

369
00:21:57,060 --> 00:21:59,380
They will break down all the starch, which

370
00:21:59,480 --> 00:22:01,480
is the food store of the grain.

371
00:22:01,580 --> 00:22:03,680
After germination, the barley

372
00:22:03,780 --> 00:22:08,080
is introduced to its famous companion, peat.

373
00:22:08,081 --> 00:22:12,160
Peat is a fuel source formed by decaying plant matter.

374
00:22:12,260 --> 00:22:17,260
It remains the primary flavor catalyst in single malt scotch.

375
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:20,160
So when you dig it and dry it out,

376
00:22:20,260 --> 00:22:24,200
you've got something that's almost like stone that burns

377
00:22:24,300 --> 00:22:26,600
with a lovely fragrance and with some heat.

378
00:22:29,460 --> 00:22:32,300
The barley is spread evenly on the floor of a kiln.

379
00:22:38,080 --> 00:22:42,680
The peat fire smokes the barley for flavor and, in the process,

380
00:22:42,681 --> 00:22:44,120
stops the germination.

381
00:22:44,121 --> 00:22:48,320
All that smoke will be drawn slowly up through the bed of

382
00:22:48,321 --> 00:22:51,320
grain, but as the smoke is actually rising through the

383
00:22:51,321 --> 00:22:54,840
bed of grain, the grain is absorbing that smoky character,

384
00:22:54,841 --> 00:22:58,440
and that ultimately makes the character of Laphroaig.

385
00:22:58,441 --> 00:23:02,080
When the malted barley is finally smoked and dried,

386
00:23:02,081 --> 00:23:05,480
it's ground and introduced to Scotch whisky's other crucial

387
00:23:05,481 --> 00:23:08,680
other crucial ingredient.

388
00:23:08,780 --> 00:23:13,280
The water is so important, it almost defines the flavor

389
00:23:13,380 --> 00:23:16,020
that is possible to create in a distillery.

390
00:23:16,120 --> 00:23:19,220
It's not the only factor, but if you don't have the right

391
00:23:19,320 --> 00:23:23,840
quality of water, no matter what size of still or what apparatus

392
00:23:23,940 --> 00:23:28,340
you set up here, you won't get a good whiskey.

393
00:23:28,440 --> 00:23:31,180
The local water is the reason many distilleries in Scotland

394
00:23:31,280 --> 00:23:35,480
have been at the same location for generations.

395
00:23:35,481 --> 00:23:37,420
By combining the perfect water source

396
00:23:37,520 --> 00:23:41,420
with the perfect pot still, a unique whiskey emerges.

397
00:23:41,520 --> 00:23:44,720
Every distillery has their own particular design of still.

398
00:23:44,820 --> 00:23:47,820
Ours here, they're very small in comparison to some you'll see.

399
00:23:47,920 --> 00:23:48,620
Very squat.

400
00:23:51,320 --> 00:23:52,840
In the Scottish Highlands region

401
00:23:52,940 --> 00:23:56,140
on the famous River Spey, the Tormor distillery

402
00:23:56,240 --> 00:24:01,380
uses a different strategy, very large stills.

403
00:24:01,381 --> 00:24:07,320
In practice, we know after 200 or 300 years of trial and error,

404
00:24:07,720 --> 00:24:10,460
if we put small stills on certain locations,

405
00:24:10,700 --> 00:24:11,900
we get wonderful whiskeys.

406
00:24:12,520 --> 00:24:15,520
But in other locations, it's the big stills that work best.

407
00:24:17,240 --> 00:24:19,040
To further refine the spirit,

408
00:24:19,400 --> 00:24:22,800
a unique purifier is positioned at the top of the Tormor stills.

409
00:24:23,700 --> 00:24:29,540
It actually helps to stop any of the rougher spirit elements

410
00:24:29,541 --> 00:24:31,040
from going through to the condenser.

411
00:24:31,380 --> 00:24:33,420
It's like a filter in a way.

412
00:24:33,520 --> 00:24:35,880
It's actually very good, but it adds body and character

413
00:24:35,980 --> 00:24:39,120
to the spirit that we have here at Tormor.

414
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:41,860
Deeper into the Speyside farmlands,

415
00:24:41,960 --> 00:24:44,620
Glendronach is the only distillery in Scotland

416
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:46,600
that clings to an ancient still technology.

417
00:24:49,460 --> 00:24:53,240
While others heat the stills by piping in hot steam,

418
00:24:53,340 --> 00:24:57,480
Glendronach practices direct firing with coal.

419
00:24:57,580 --> 00:25:00,140
It produces particular compounds

420
00:25:00,141 --> 00:25:02,980
the whiskey as it's being distilled that aren't produced

421
00:25:03,080 --> 00:25:05,840
when the heat is generated within the still from a steam

422
00:25:05,940 --> 00:25:08,380
coil or some other means.

423
00:25:08,480 --> 00:25:10,620
While the shape of the still and the heat source

424
00:25:10,720 --> 00:25:14,520
have influence, barrel aging is what defines the flavor of

425
00:25:14,620 --> 00:25:16,860
single malt scotch.

426
00:25:16,960 --> 00:25:20,200
And in Scotland, it's a long wait.

427
00:25:20,300 --> 00:25:23,800
Single malts are routinely aged for 12, 15, and sometimes even

428
00:25:23,900 --> 00:25:25,460
30 and 40 years.

429
00:25:25,461 --> 00:25:29,360
years.

430
00:25:29,460 --> 00:25:32,260
As the barrels age, they absorb the character of the air

431
00:25:32,360 --> 00:25:35,400
and the land around them.

432
00:25:35,500 --> 00:25:38,900
In exchange, the whiskey barrel gives something back.

433
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,800
It's called the angel share.

434
00:25:41,900 --> 00:25:48,820
In the cask, we are losing 2% of the alcohol per year.

435
00:25:48,920 --> 00:25:51,460
2% of the volume disappears.

436
00:25:51,560 --> 00:25:54,720
So what is happening as the whiskey evaporates

437
00:25:54,721 --> 00:25:57,760
is the volume inside the cast goes down,

438
00:25:57,860 --> 00:26:01,320
and the angels get their reward.

439
00:26:01,420 --> 00:26:04,960
The worldwide sales of single malt are exploding.

440
00:26:05,060 --> 00:26:07,260
But it remains only a fraction of the world's supply

441
00:26:07,360 --> 00:26:09,560
of Scotch whiskey.

442
00:26:09,660 --> 00:26:13,480
Most Scotch is blended, representing over 90%

443
00:26:13,580 --> 00:26:15,780
of all Scotch whiskey.

444
00:26:15,880 --> 00:26:19,440
In blended Scotch whiskeys, many single malts

445
00:26:19,540 --> 00:26:24,720
are put into a whiskey vatting along with grain whiskeys.

446
00:26:24,721 --> 00:26:30,720
and that's blended whiskey. Green whiskies made from corn

447
00:26:30,721 --> 00:26:34,320
or unmalted barley are used to soften the single malt scotches,

448
00:26:34,321 --> 00:26:38,320
making most blended scotches less expensive and more appealing to a

449
00:26:38,321 --> 00:26:43,520
mass market. Whether blended or single malt,

450
00:26:43,521 --> 00:26:47,920
scotch is the second largest export in Scotland and a source

451
00:26:47,921 --> 00:26:52,320
of intense national pride. On the other side of the world,

452
00:26:52,321 --> 00:26:55,920
There is a wild spirit distilled from a wicked-looking plant

453
00:26:55,921 --> 00:27:01,520
that inspires its own passionate following.

454
00:27:01,521 --> 00:27:04,640
For hundreds of years, acceptable alcohol content,

455
00:27:04,641 --> 00:27:08,400
or proof, was measured when whiskey and gunpowder were

456
00:27:08,401 --> 00:27:12,080
mixed and ignited. If the gunpowder flashed,

457
00:27:12,081 --> 00:27:18,080
the alcohol content was proved.

458
00:27:18,081 --> 00:27:21,920
In the heart of San Francisco, Tommy's Mexican Restaurant is

459
00:27:21,921 --> 00:27:25,920
is regularly packed with a devoted crowd.

460
00:27:25,921 --> 00:27:28,960
They come to experience the best the tequila distilleries

461
00:27:28,961 --> 00:27:30,560
have to offer.

462
00:27:30,561 --> 00:27:33,880
At Tommie's, all of the tequilas are distilled entirely

463
00:27:33,881 --> 00:27:37,580
from agave, a relative of the cactus.

464
00:27:37,581 --> 00:27:41,360
At Tommie's, we've taken a conscious stand

465
00:27:41,361 --> 00:27:48,280
to only serve arguably the best tequila, which is 100% agave

466
00:27:48,281 --> 00:27:48,981
tequila.

467
00:27:51,920 --> 00:27:56,520
The Margarita, made with the spirit of Mexico,

468
00:27:56,680 --> 00:28:00,780
100% blue agave tequila and fresh lime juice.

469
00:28:01,220 --> 00:28:02,920
Three-quarters of a part of the sour ingredient,

470
00:28:03,060 --> 00:28:03,780
fresh lime juice.

471
00:28:04,140 --> 00:28:06,340
One part of the sweet ingredient, orange liqueur.

472
00:28:07,720 --> 00:28:12,840
And 100% blue agave tequila.

473
00:28:15,420 --> 00:28:16,800
And let's shake it up.

474
00:28:16,801 --> 00:28:20,420
It is truly one of the most popular cocktails

475
00:28:20,421 --> 00:28:21,640
in the world today.

476
00:28:21,920 --> 00:28:26,920
Margarita.

477
00:28:26,921 --> 00:28:29,920
What a beautiful drink.

478
00:28:29,921 --> 00:28:31,920
Tequila distilleries are centered

479
00:28:31,921 --> 00:28:35,920
in only one region of the world, the warm mountainous area

480
00:28:35,921 --> 00:28:39,920
of Jalisco, Mexico, where the blue agave grows.

481
00:28:39,921 --> 00:28:43,920
While there are over 400 species of agave, by law,

482
00:28:43,921 --> 00:28:46,920
only the juice of the blue agave can be used

483
00:28:46,921 --> 00:28:48,920
for distilling tequila.

484
00:28:48,921 --> 00:28:53,360
can be used for distilling tequila.

485
00:28:53,361 --> 00:28:57,660
Its seductive flavor has been known for hundreds of years.

486
00:28:57,661 --> 00:29:01,220
The Aztec Indians fermented the agave pulp into a beverage

487
00:29:01,221 --> 00:29:04,920
called pulque. The plant was considered sacred,

488
00:29:04,921 --> 00:29:08,320
and pulque was used in religious ceremonies.

489
00:29:08,321 --> 00:29:11,920
With the arrival of the Spaniards in Mexico in the

490
00:29:11,921 --> 00:29:15,480
16th century, they brought distillation techniques and

491
00:29:15,481 --> 00:29:20,480
that the natives were using, and that's how tequila came out.

492
00:29:20,481 --> 00:29:23,480
Today, nearly a quarter of a million acres of blue agave

493
00:29:23,481 --> 00:29:28,480
are waiting to become tequila in Mexico's nearly 100 distilleries.

494
00:29:28,481 --> 00:29:32,480
The plant takes as long as 12 years to reach maturity before

495
00:29:32,481 --> 00:29:38,480
it can be harvested. The dangerous art of the jimador,

496
00:29:38,481 --> 00:29:42,480
or the agave harvester, is a crucial skill in tequila

497
00:29:42,481 --> 00:29:46,020
is a crucial skill in tequila production.

498
00:29:46,120 --> 00:29:48,520
Using an implement called the koa,

499
00:29:48,620 --> 00:29:50,720
the workers shave the razor-sharp leaves

500
00:29:50,820 --> 00:29:54,220
close to the stem, called a piña.

501
00:29:54,320 --> 00:29:56,420
This is a piña of agave.

502
00:29:56,520 --> 00:30:00,040
As you can see here, we have the heart of the agave,

